title: 'Restricted-skgrepo nodes: omission, retention, and save preservation'
pid: 31644a79-ec42-4a40-a005-1549c8ccb763
body: |2-
    - The principle: the skgrepo restriction limits writes as it limits
      views. Under a skgrepo restriction, no save or other
      write removes, moves, rewrites or creates a record, a section or
      a node file the set keeps out, and none reads the buffer's silence
      about such a record as an edit: what the user could not see, the
      user did not edit. The rules below are its cases.

  Under a skgrepo restriction, a node whose home skgrepo the set
  keeps out is /restricted/, and the view shows nothing of it: no
  title, no body, no home skgrepo, no stats. What it shows where a
  relationship names such a node depends on the relationship's
  /record/, the relationship as one node's file states it, in one
  skgrepo's section, its relRepo.

  A member whose record the set keeps out is omitted entirely --
  content, forkees, and every partner folder's members alike.
  Omission is recursive: an omitted container's whole branch is
  omitted, unrestricted descendants included (they stay reachable by
  search or direct visit). A member whose record the set shows, and
  whose node it keeps out, stands as a /restricted viewnode/, which
  carries the record's gnid and relRepo and nothing of the node:

  #+begin_example
  (skg restricted (gnid ID) [(skgRepos (relRepo R))] [noClaim] [(editRequest ...)])
  #+end_example

  Its herald is "restricted". It has no content of its own drawn: no
  goal, no folders. It claims membership as an unknown viewnode does, takes a
  relRepo request and a processed request, and refuses text typed on
  its line. A member whose record the set shows and that resolves to
  no node at all is an unknown viewnode, as under no skgrepo restriction. A view rooted
  at a node the set keeps out (one opened before a skgrepo narrowing;
  opening one under the skgrepo restriction is refused) shows its root
  as a restricted viewnode too, whether or not a record names it,
  since the view is the user's own request; it is the node's occurrence again after a
  skgrepo widening. An alias whose record the set
  keeps out is neither drawn nor counted in =(props (aliases N))=.
  The diff view opens only on an unrestricted connection, but a
  switch rerenders its buffers under the new set as a content view
  is rerendered: a restricted node is never a ghost there.

  Saving preserves what rendering omitted: a member whose record is
  restricted cannot be deleted (or even seen), so the save combines the
  buffer's visible lists with the disk lists -- order-preserving (the
  anchored weave) for =contains= and =forks= alike -- restoring only
  the members whose records are
  restricted, and a node's aliases whose records are restricted. A
  restricted viewnode keeps its member in the list while
  it claims, and deleting its line removes the record, as deleting an
  unknown viewnode's does, but in the forks folder: there deleting a
  restricted viewnode changes no relationship to the note it stands
  for, so the save keeps N's fork of it, hides nothing, and the
  rerender draws it again. The folder of what a note hides outside its
  fork partners (the members its forks folder shows, forkees,
  forkers and indirect members) replaces the hides with a visible record, each of which
  it showed, as an occurrence or an unknown viewnode; a
  hide recorded in a restricted section is never replaced, and
  deleting a restricted viewnode there unhides nothing. Which hides
  stand outside the fork partners is what the visible records say: a
  hide of a node a partner contains only by a restricted record
  stands in that folder, and its line edits the hide. A forkee
  shown editable under its forker shows its content by its
  visible records, less the hides with a visible record; the save
  writes the forker's hides from what it shows, by the same
  records, so that a hide recorded in a restricted section survives
  even where the forkee's content shows the hidden node, and no
  hide is written of a member only a restricted record explains. Edits to
  visible members, including deletions, are honored. A member whose
  record is restricted needs no headline for this: the disk supplies
  it.

  When a switch of the skgrepo restriction is applied to an open view (an
  already-rendered buffer), every occurrence of a now-restricted node
  becomes, in place, a restricted viewnode where its record is visible,
  keeping the occurrence's viewnode ID, fold state and viewchildren, so
  its visible descendants keep their place, and so does a view root
  whether or not its record is visible; otherwise it vanishes, with
  its subtree, so nothing the skgrepo restriction keeps out leaks. So does a member whose record alone the set keeps out, where
  its parent reads claims: the graph as the set shows it no longer
  relates it to its parent. Neither is a detachment: a restricted
  record's membership in its parent's list is owned entirely by disk
  supplementation, not the buffer. The folders of a node that became a
  restricted viewnode, like its property members and change viewnodes,
  cannot stand under it: each becomes a tombstone holding what stood
  under it, a childless one is pruned, and the tombstones stay after
  the switch back. An editable folder whose every member's record the
  new set keeps out stays, empty. The children of a restricted viewnode
  remain occurrences, claiming nothing. Switching back makes each
  restricted viewnode an occurrence again, where it stands, its children
  claiming again where the node's lists name them, and draws again each
  node and member that vanished, where the graph puts it. A de-novo
  render under a skgrepo restriction draws no such member in the first
  place -- members whose records are restricted are omitted and their
  content is never extended.

  Edits to restricted nodes themselves are /suppressed/ at save, not
  fatal: any nodeInstruction that would write a restricted skgrepo is
  dropped and the save succeeds with the warning "Restricted nodes
  present in saved buffer remain unchanged in graph." A node is
  restricted by its home as the graph has it, as well as by the home
  the buffer gives it: a headline for a restricted graphnode, pasted
  with a visible nodeRepo, leaves the graphnode where it is, its title
  and body as they were. A node, restricted or not, whose buffer text
  matches the graph is not written at all: its nodeInstruction is
  discarded as a no-op first, so it saves silently however old the
  buffer. A node the buffer would write that changed after the buffer
  was rendered refuses the whole save ("Save buffer", the stale
  buffer); a suppressed write to a restricted node is dropped before
  that check, so it refuses nothing. A
  nodeMerge of a restricted node is suppressed the same way.

  The writes that are not edits of a restricted node obey the
  principle as follows.
    - A record that would land in a restricted section is refused, with
      nothing written, naming the recorder, the relation, the member and
      the skgrepo ("Cannot save a record into a restricted repo ...
      Activate it first; an invisible record is never created
      silently."): a member typed or pasted whose default floor, the
      more private of the two homes, is restricted; a hide inferred from
      a removal, or typed into the folder of what a note hides
      outside its fork partners, whose floor (its most public partner on
      record) lands in a restricted section; an explicit relRepo request to a restricted
      skgrepo. A record the graph already holds there is not refused.
      Nor may a record leave such a section: a headline pasted for a
      member whose record is restricted, carrying an explicit relRepo
      request, is refused ("Cannot move a record out of a restricted
      repo ...").
    - A new hide's relRepo is at least as private as some partner on
      record for it ('docs/glossary.org', "hides"): the =forks= record
      between the hider and a fork partner, either way (the hider's for
      a forkee, the forker's for a forker), and that partner's contains
      record, the more private of the two, the most public such partner
      winning; and at least the more private of the two homes. A partner
      on record is any fork partner the graph has, whatever the skgrepo
      restriction, a forker the hider hides included: the floor asks
      who can read the hide, not what a view draws. Removals infer hides only from
      what the set shows: the note's visible forkees, forkers and
      indirect members (less those it hides), their contents by visible
      records, and its visible hides, so that re-adding a member drops no restricted
      hide of it.
    - A fork copies into its clone only the records the set shows, each
      at the more private of its relRepo, when owned, and the clone's
      home. The rest stays the original's; the clone forks it,
      so after a skgrepo widening the rest shows under the clone as
      unintegrated content.
    - Deleting a node whose telescope has a restricted section is
      refused ("Cannot delete 'ID': it has telescope sections in
      restricted repos. ..."), and so is a nodeMerge either of whose
      nodes has one ("Cannot merge 'B' into 'A': ..."). A delete whose
      propagation reaches restricted records of other nodes stops for
      the delete-from confirmation ("Save buffer").
    - The cleanup of the references to an absent ID, and a save's
      restore and processed requests, are refused unless the connection
      is unrestricted. The import of md and org files is refused when
      its destination skgrepo is restricted.
